I'll refactor the ChatModeSelector to use a `<select>` element instead of a dropdown menu. I'll maintain the existing functionality while simplifying the component structure.

```vue src/components/chat/ChatModeSelector.vue
<script setup>
import ChatIcon from './ChatIcon.vue'
</script>

<template>
  <div class="w-full">
    <select 
      class="select select-ghost w-full"
      :value="selectedMode"
      @change="selectMode"
    >
      <option v-for="mode in availableModes" :key="mode" :value="mode">
        <span class="capitalize">{{ mode }}</span>
      </option>
    </select>
  </div>
</template>

<script>
export default {
  props: {
    selectedMode: {
      type: String,
      default: 'chat'
    }
  },
  emits: ['mode-changed'],
  data() {
    return {
      availableModes: ['chat', 'task', 'topic', 'prview', 'browser', 'slides']
    }
  },
  methods: {
    selectMode(event) {
      const mode = event.target.value
      if (mode !== this.selectedMode) {
        this.$emit('mode-changed', mode)
      }
    }
  }
}
</script>
```

## Changes Summary

| Change | Details |
|--------|---------|
| **Template** | Replaced DaisyUI dropdown with `<select>` element |
| **Select Element** | Uses `select-ghost` class to match previous styling |
| **Value Binding** | Added `:value` binding to sync with `selectedMode` prop |
| **Options** | Looped through `availableModes` to generate options |
| **Event Handler** | Updated `selectMode()` to handle change event directly |
| **Removed** | ChatIcon from dropdown trigger (simplified UI), alert() call |
| **Simplified** | Removed nested click handlers and menu structure |

The component now uses a native HTML select with DaisyUI styling, maintaining the same functionality while being cleaner and more accessible.
